// lsp.json: the JSON reader and writer of the language server.
// D20.5
//
// A reader parses a document into a tagged tree of nodes owned by one arena,
// which the caller frees; a writer builds a document in a std.strbuf buffer,
// as src/fort/json.ft does for the check mode. The two halves meet in
// write_value, which writes a parsed tree back, so a document that this module
// reads is a document it can write.
//
// ---- why this is a second JSON module ----
//
// src/fort/json.ft is the compiler's writer and stays the compiler's. Three
// reasons keep them apart. The failure policies differ: a violated
// precondition panics there because the compiler builds its own document and
// cannot write a bad one, while a server reads bytes an editor sent and must
// answer a malformed document with an error record. The compiler's module is
// inside the bootstrap fixed point (tools/fixpoint.sh, ctest bootstrap) and
// inside the module diff_ir.sh compares, so a reader added there would grow
// the compiler with code the compiler never calls and would re-enter the fixed
// point for every change the server makes. And a module's identity is the real
// path of its file while a symbol is mangled from its module path, so two
// files that are both the module `json` in one closure emit the same symbols:
// this file is reached through the search root `src` and is therefore
// `lsp.json`, which no compiler module spells.
// D13.3, D20.5, D9.1, D9.7
//
// The price of the split is the writer, which is this file's copy of what
// src/fort/json.ft writes, the escape rule and the UTF-8 validation included.
// test/fort/lsp_json_agreement_test.ft holds the two writers against each
// other byte for byte over the same calls, so the copy cannot drift in
// silence. That test is the reason the copy is acceptable.
//
// An lsp module never imports the compiler's `json`. The directory holding
// the entry file is the first search root, so `import json;` in a server
// whose entry file stands in src/lsp reaches this file rather than the
// compiler's, and that would give one file two module paths, which is an
// error. The server writes its JSON with this module and with no other.
// D9.2
//
// ---- the arena ----
//
// A doc owns every node and every byte of text it hands out, and json.free
// releases all of it. The nodes live in chunks of NODE_CHUNK that never move,
// which is the block arena of src/bootstrap/ast.h. A chunk is never copied,
// so the arena grows by exactly one chunk at a time rather than doubling a
// vector and copying it. A doubling vector of nodes would hold as much as
// twice the nodes it uses and, while it copies, three times; a chunk list
// holds the nodes and the tail of one chunk. A handle here is an index, where
// the C arena hands out a pointer, so the chunk of a node is arithmetic on the
// handle and is held nowhere. src/fort/ast.ft keeps neither shape: its arena
// owns one pointer per node, and the comment above its `arena` says why the
// block bookkeeping went and what deferring it costs.
//
// The text of every string and every number is copied into one buffer whose
// capacity is reserved once, at the length of the source: decoding never makes
// a string longer (`\n` is two bytes and yields one, a surrogate pair is
// twelve and yields four), a number is copied verbatim, the tokens are
// disjoint,
// so the decoded bytes of a whole document never exceed its source. The buffer
// therefore never grows during a parse and never holds more than one document.
//
// json.clear empties a doc and keeps its chunks, so a server that parses a
// thousand messages through one doc allocates for the largest of them and not
// for their sum.
// D20.5
//
// ---- numbers ----
//
// A number is k_int when it is written without a fraction and without an
// exponent and its value fits i64, and k_float otherwise. Both keep the source
// text, which is what json.text_of answers and what write_value writes back:
// JSON fixes no canonical spelling of a number, and the compiler's shortest
// round-trip text would rewrite 1.50 as 1.5, so the text is the only
// byte-stable answer.
// json.int_of reads the text with std.str.parse_i64 and json.float_of reads
// it with flt.flt_parse, the compiler's correctly rounded decimal to binary64
// conversion, which answers the bits of an f64 rather than an f64.
// This module names no float type, so the C bootstrap compiles it and the
// four differential oracles of notes/testing.md 5 cover it; a caller that
// wants the value itself copies the bits into an f64 with libc.memcpy, the
// bit cast std.rt_float makes in the other direction.
// D11.7, D18.1
//
// ---- what the reader refuses ----
//
// The grammar of RFC 8259 and three rules beside it: a document deeper than
// JSON_MAX_DEPTH, a string that is not well-formed UTF-8, and a source longer
// than the cap json.set_max_bytes fixes. Nothing else is refused. A number too
// large for binary64 reads as an infinity and a number too small reads as a
// zero, which is what every other JSON reader answers, and neither is a
// document error. Every refusal is an error record: json.error_offset is the
// 0-based byte offset of the byte that failed and json.error_message names the
// rule. The reader reports every document it refuses; it panics over none of
// them.
//
// ---- the cap, and the one way a document still ends the process ----
//
// An allocation that fails is a runtime error and ends the program. It is not
// a panic and no library can answer it, so the only defence a reader has is a
// bound on what a document may ask for. The cap is that bound: a source above
// it is an error record before a byte of it is read, and a source at it asks
// for at most JSON_ARENA_BOUND times its length (see the arena above). The
// default is JSON_MAX_BYTES, sixteen mebibytes, which bounds one document at
// about 320 mebibytes; json.set_max_bytes moves it, and JSON_BYTES_CEILING is the
// largest value it takes, since a node holds its text as a u32 offset. A
// server that reads a Content-Length header knows the size before it parses
// and can refuse a message earlier still.
// D10.2
//
// A violated precondition of the API is a panic, as everywhere else: asking a
// string for its int value, or an array for a child it does not have, is a
// caller that did not check the kind first. A query that asks what is there
// answers for every kind instead: count_of, first_of, next_of and member_of
// answer 0 for a value that has no such child, so a server may walk a message
// it has not checked. A query that names a child which must exist panics:
// at_of past the child count, and a handle no parse handed out.
// D13.3
//
// Fifteen calls of panic stand in this file and twelve tests hold one each:
//   grep -c '^ *panic("' src/lsp/json.ft        reads 15
//   ls test/fort/lsp_json_*panic_test.ft | wc -l  reads 12
// One test for each, since a panic ends the program and one program can show
// one panic. The three without a test are unreachable and stand as the
// statement of an invariant: chunks_reserve overflows only above half the
// address space, int_of contradicts the reader's own classification of the
// text it made a k_int, and write_value meets the absent node, which node_of
// has already refused. A panic added here is a test added beside it, and those
// two commands are what a reader holds that against. They are a count and not
// a test: nothing in the gate fails when the two numbers part, which is the
// one place this module is weaker than its error records, where
// lsp_json_corpus_test.ft holds all 19 against the corpus.
//
// Re-entrancy: no module-level mutable state. A doc and a writer are values
// the caller owns, every allocation belongs to the doc that made it, and
// nothing here ends the process over an input.
// D20.5
//
// Dispatch: none. The reader is a recursive descent over a switch and the
// writer a chain of comparisons over one byte, as src/fort/json.ft writes it,
// and not a table of function pointers.
// D3.10
import flt;
import std.str;
import std.strbuf;

/// The deepest nesting of arrays and objects the reader accepts and the writer
/// allows. An LSP message nests about ten deep at its worst, the capabilities
/// object of `initialize`; sixty-four leaves room for a document a server did
/// not write and bounds the reader's recursion, which is one frame per level.
u64 JSON_MAX_DEPTH = 64;

/// The longest document a doc accepts until json.set_max_bytes says otherwise.
/// Sixteen mebibytes is above any message an editor sends -- the largest of
/// them carries one source file -- and bounds the arena of one document at
/// about 320 MB, which a server survives. A document above the cap is an error
/// record and not an allocation that fails.
/// D10.2
u64 JSON_MAX_BYTES = 16777216;

/// The largest cap json.set_max_bytes takes. A node holds its text as a u32
/// offset and a u32 length, so no offset of a document may exceed this; a cap
/// above it is a violated precondition and panics.
/// D13.3
u64 JSON_BYTES_CEILING = 4294967295;

/// What the arena of a document costs, as a multiple of the source: the bound
/// test/fort/lsp_json_arena_test.ft holds the module to, and the number the cap
/// above is chosen against. The header of this file derives it.
u64 JSON_ARENA_BOUND = 20;

/// The nodes of one chunk, and the shift and the mask that split a handle into
/// a chunk index and a slot. A chunk is the floor of the arena, since a
/// document of one value still takes one: 256 nodes of 36 bytes and the
/// 16-byte record that owns them are 9232 bytes, which is what arena_bytes
/// counts for a chunk and what test/fort/lsp_json_arena_test.ft asserts. That
/// is small enough for a server that parses a keystroke and large enough that
/// a document of half a million values takes 2048 chunk pointers. A chunk
/// stays below the 128 KB at which glibc serves an allocation by mmap, which is
/// what keeps a leak of one visible to the probes of notes/testing.md 4.
u64 NODE_CHUNK = 256;
u64 NODE_CHUNK_SHIFT = 8;
u64 NODE_CHUNK_MASK = 255;

/// The first non-zero capacity of the chunk vector; it doubles from there. The
/// vector holds one pointer per NODE_CHUNK nodes, so it is never the arena's
/// cost.
u64 CHUNK_MIN_CAP = 4;

/// The largest u64 there is, named once so that the capacity guard holds its
/// arithmetic against one spelling of it.
/// D1.4
u64 U64_MAX = 18446744073709551615;

/// The hex digits of a \u00XX escape, uppercase as src/fort/json.ft writes
/// them, and the shift and the mask that pick the two digits of one byte out.
string HEX_DIGITS = "0123456789ABCDEF";
u64 HEX_SHIFT = 4;
u64 HEX_MASK = 15;

/// The bytes the escape rule names: `"` and `\`, which are escaped, and the
/// three control bytes JSON spells out.
u64 QUOTE_BYTE = 34;
u64 BACKSLASH_BYTE = 92;
u64 NEWLINE_BYTE = 10;
u64 TAB_BYTE = 9;
u64 RETURN_BYTE = 13;

/// The last control byte: a byte at or below it is escaped.
u64 CONTROL_MAX = 31;

/// The last ASCII byte, and the byte ranges of a well-formed UTF-8 sequence
/// (RFC 3629), as src/fort/json.ft names them: a lead byte of two, three or
/// four bytes, the continuation bytes that follow it, and the four second-byte
/// ranges that rule out an overlong form, a UTF-16 surrogate and a value past
/// U+10FFFF.
u64 ASCII_MAX = 127;
u64 UTF8_CONT_MIN = 128;
u64 UTF8_CONT_MAX = 191;
u64 UTF8_LEAD2_MIN = 194;
u64 UTF8_LEAD2_MAX = 223;
u64 UTF8_LEAD3_MIN = 224;
u64 UTF8_LEAD3_MAX = 239;
u64 UTF8_LEAD4_MIN = 240;
u64 UTF8_LEAD4_MAX = 244;
u64 UTF8_LEAD3_OVERLONG = 224;
u64 UTF8_LEAD3_SURROGATE = 237;
u64 UTF8_LEAD4_OVERLONG = 240;
u64 UTF8_LEAD4_HIGHEST = 244;
u64 UTF8_E0_MIN = 160;
u64 UTF8_ED_MAX = 159;
u64 UTF8_F0_MIN = 144;
u64 UTF8_F4_MAX = 143;

/// The bytes of U+FFFD, the replacement character a byte that is part of no
/// well-formed sequence is written as, so that a document the writer builds is
/// valid UTF-8 whatever its caller held.
string REPLACEMENT = "\xEF\xBF\xBD";

/// Encoding a code point the \uXXXX escapes of a string decode to: the lead
/// byte of each length, the continuation lead, and the six bits a continuation
/// byte carries.
u64 UTF8_ONE_MAX = 127;
u64 UTF8_TWO_MAX = 2047;
u64 UTF8_THREE_MAX = 65535;
u64 UTF8_CONT_LEAD = 128;
u64 UTF8_TWO_LEAD = 192;
u64 UTF8_THREE_LEAD = 224;
u64 UTF8_FOUR_LEAD = 240;
u64 UTF8_CONT_BITS = 6;
u64 UTF8_CONT_VALUE_MASK = 63;

/// The UTF-16 surrogates of a \uXXXX pair (RFC 8259 7): a high surrogate, a low
/// surrogate, and the arithmetic that joins them into a code point.
u64 SURROGATE_HIGH_MIN = 55296;
u64 SURROGATE_HIGH_MAX = 56319;
u64 SURROGATE_LOW_MIN = 56320;
u64 SURROGATE_LOW_MAX = 57343;
u64 SURROGATE_SHIFT = 10;
u64 SURROGATE_BASE = 65536;

/// The four hex digits of a \uXXXX escape and the base they are read in.
u64 ESCAPE_HEX_DIGITS = 4;
u64 HEX_BASE = 16;

// ---- the tree --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The kinds of a JSON value. k_none is the kind of handle 0, which is the
/// absent handle: no node the reader builds ever carries it.
enum value_kind {
    k_none,
    k_null,
    k_bool,
    k_int,
    k_float,
    k_string,
    k_array,
    k_object,
}

/// One value. `at` and `len` are the offset and the length in the doc's byte
/// buffer of a string's decoded bytes or of a number's source text; `key_at`
/// and `key_len` are the same for the name of a member of an object, empty on
/// every other node; `first` and `next` thread the children of an array or an
/// object, both 0 at the end of a list; `count` is how many children there are;
/// `bval` is the value of a k_bool node. A node of 36 bytes is what bounds the
/// arena, so a field added here is paid for by every value of every document.
struct node {
    value_kind kind;
    bool bval;
    u32 at;
    u32 len;
    u32 key_at;
    u32 key_len;
    u32 first;
    u32 next;
    u32 count;
}

/// One chunk of NODE_CHUNK nodes. The wrapper exists because a span of spans is
/// a nested level the C bootstrap refuses, and because a chunk that owns its
/// slots releases them with itself.
/// D3.6
struct node_chunk {
    node mut@ own slots;
}

/// A parsed document and the arena that owns it. doc{} is a valid empty
/// document, so a struct holding one is initialized with {}; json.create
/// returns the same value. `count` counts handle 0 as well, so the first node
/// the reader builds is handle 1.
struct doc {
    node_chunk mut* own mut@ own chunks;
    u64 chunk_count;
    u64 count;
    strbuf.str_buf bytes;
    u32 root_node;
    bool failed;
    u64 error_at;
    string error_msg;
    /// The cap on a source, 0 meaning JSON_MAX_BYTES. A zero stands for the
    /// default so that doc{} is a document with the default cap and not one
    /// that refuses every source; json.free and json.clear leave it alone,
    /// since the cap belongs to the caller and not to the document.
    u64 limit;
}

/// The empty document; allocates nothing. A call result needs no move.
/// D17.5
fn doc create() {
    return doc{};
}

/// The cap on the length of a source this document parses. json.create leaves
/// the default, JSON_MAX_BYTES.
fn u64 max_bytes(doc* d) {
    if (d->limit == 0) {
        return JSON_MAX_BYTES;
    }
    return d->limit;
}

/// Moves the cap: a source longer than `limit` is refused with an error record
/// before it is read. 0 restores JSON_MAX_BYTES. A limit above
/// JSON_BYTES_CEILING is a violated precondition and panics, since a node
/// could not then hold the offset of its own text.
/// D13.3
///
/// The cap is what stands between a document and the allocator: a source of
/// `limit` bytes asks for at most JSON_ARENA_BOUND times that, and an
/// allocation that fails ends the program, which no error record can answer.
/// D10.2
fn void set_max_bytes(doc mut* d, u64 limit) {
    if (limit > JSON_BYTES_CEILING) {
        panic("json.set_max_bytes: above the ceiling");
    }
    d->limit = limit;
}

/// Releases every chunk, the chunk vector and the byte buffer: every node and
/// every string this document handed out dies here, and the document is empty
/// and usable afterwards. del empties an operand, so a second call, or a call
/// on doc{}, is a no-op.
/// D17.9
fn void free(doc mut* d) {
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < d->chunk_count) {
        // The chunk owns its slots and del is shallow, so the slots go before
        // the chunk does.
        // D17.7
        node_chunk mut* own chunk = move(d->chunks[i]);
        del(chunk->slots);
        del(chunk);
        i += 1;
    }
    del(d->chunks);
    d->chunk_count = 0;
    strbuf.free(&d->bytes);
    reset(d);
}

/// Empties the document and keeps its chunks and its byte buffer, so that the
/// next parse reuses the storage of this one. The handles and the strings of
/// the document that was there die here.
/// D20.5
fn void clear(doc mut* d) {
    strbuf.clear(&d->bytes);
    reset(d);
}

/// The state both free and clear leave: no nodes, no root, no error.
fn void reset(doc mut* d) {
    d->count = 0;
    d->root_node = 0;
    d->failed = false;
    d->error_at = 0;
    d->error_msg = "";
}

/// Ensures the chunk vector holds one more chunk, doubling from CHUNK_MIN_CAP.
fn void chunks_reserve(doc mut* d) {
    if (d->chunk_count < d->chunks.len) {
        return;
    }
    u64 mut cap = CHUNK_MIN_CAP;
    if (d->chunks.len >= cap) {
        if (d->chunks.len > U64_MAX / 2) {
            panic("json.chunks_reserve: capacity overflow");
        }
        cap = d->chunks.len * 2;
    }
    node_chunk mut* own mut@ own bigger = new(node_chunk mut* own, cap);
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < d->chunk_count) {
        bigger[i] = move(d->chunks[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    del(d->chunks);
    d->chunks = move(bigger);
}

/// Adds one chunk of NODE_CHUNK nodes at the end of the chunk vector.
fn void add_chunk(doc mut* d) {
    chunks_reserve(d);
    node_chunk mut* own chunk = new(node_chunk);
    chunk->slots = new(node, NODE_CHUNK);
    d->chunks[d->chunk_count] = move(chunk);
    d->chunk_count += 1;
}

/// A zeroed node of `kind`, and its handle. Handle 0 is built first and is the
/// absent node, which is why the reader's first value is handle 1.
fn u32 new_node(doc mut* d, value_kind kind) {
    u64 chunk = d->count >> NODE_CHUNK_SHIFT;
    if (chunk >= d->chunk_count) {
        add_chunk(d);
    }
    node mut* fresh = node_at(d, d->count);
    *fresh = node{};
    fresh->kind = kind;
    u32 handle = cast(d->count, u32);
    d->count += 1;
    return handle;
}

/// The node at index `i` of the arena, a view of the chunk that owns
/// it. The index must be below the nodes handed out.
/// D17.3
fn node mut* node_at(doc mut* d, u64 i) {
    node_chunk mut* chunk = d->chunks[i >> NODE_CHUNK_SHIFT];
    return &chunk->slots[i & NODE_CHUNK_MASK];
}

/// The same over a document nothing is writing, which every query uses.
fn node* node_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    if (v == 0 || cast(v, u64) >= d->count) {
        panic("json.node_of: not a handle of this document");
    }
    node_chunk* chunk = d->chunks[cast(v, u64) >> NODE_CHUNK_SHIFT];
    return &chunk->slots[cast(v, u64) & NODE_CHUNK_MASK];
}

// ---- queries ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The root value of the last parse, or 0 when no parse succeeded.
fn u32 root(doc* d) {
    return d->root_node;
}

/// Whether `v` names a value of this document. Handle 0 names none.
fn bool has(doc* d, u32 v) {
    return v != 0 && cast(v, u64) < d->count;
}

/// The kind of `v`; a handle this document did not hand out is a violated
/// precondition and panics.
/// D13.3
fn value_kind kind_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    return node_of(d, v)->kind;
}

/// The number of children of an array or an object; 0 for every other kind.
fn u64 count_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    return cast(node_of(d, v)->count, u64);
}

/// The first child of an array or an object, 0 when it is empty.
fn u32 first_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    return node_of(d, v)->first;
}

/// The sibling that follows `v` in its container, 0 when it is the last. The
/// pair first_of and next_of walks a container in one pass; at_of walks the
/// list again for each index, so a loop over a large array uses this pair.
fn u32 next_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    return node_of(d, v)->next;
}

/// The child at `i` of an array or an object; an index at or past the child
/// count is a violated precondition and panics. It costs one step per index,
/// so it is the query of a small container.
/// D13.3
fn u32 at_of(doc* d, u32 v, u64 i) {
    node* container = node_of(d, v);
    if (i >= cast(container->count, u64)) {
        panic("json.at_of: index out of range");
    }
    u32 mut child = container->first;
    u64 mut seen = 0;
    while (seen < i) {
        child = node_of(d, child)->next;
        seen += 1;
    }
    return child;
}

/// The member of the object `v` whose name is `name`, or 0 when it has none. A
/// value that is not an object has no member of any name and answers 0 as
/// well, which is what lets a server walk a message it has not checked: the
/// query asks what is there, as count_of and first_of do, and only a query that
/// names a child which must exist panics.
/// A document may name one member twice; this answers the first of them.
fn u32 member_of(doc* d, u32 v, string name) {
    node* object = node_of(d, v);
    if (object->kind != value_kind.k_object) {
        return 0;
    }
    u32 mut child = object->first;
    while (child != 0) {
        node* member = node_of(d, child);
        if (slice(d, member->key_at, member->key_len) == name) {
            return child;
        }
        child = member->next;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// The name of `v` as a member of its object, and the empty string when `v` is
/// not a member of an object. Ownership: a view of the document's own bytes,
/// valid until the document is freed, cleared or parsed again.
/// D17.3
fn string key_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* member = node_of(d, v);
    return slice(d, member->key_at, member->key_len);
}

/// The bytes of a string, with every escape decoded, or the source text of a
/// number, verbatim. Every other kind is a violated precondition and panics.
/// Ownership: a view, as key_of.
/// D13.3
fn string text_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* n = node_of(d, v);
    if (n->kind != value_kind.k_string && n->kind != value_kind.k_int
        && n->kind != value_kind.k_float) {
        panic("json.text_of: not a string and not a number");
    }
    return slice(d, n->at, n->len);
}

/// The value of a k_bool node; every other kind is a violated precondition and
/// panics.
/// D13.3
fn bool bool_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* n = node_of(d, v);
    if (n->kind != value_kind.k_bool) {
        panic("json.bool_of: not a boolean");
    }
    return n->bval;
}

/// The value of a k_int node, read from its text. Every other kind, k_float
/// included, is a violated precondition and panics: a caller that will take a
/// number of either kind asks for float_of first.
/// D13.3
fn i64 int_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* n = node_of(d, v);
    if (n->kind != value_kind.k_int) {
        panic("json.int_of: not an integer");
    }
    i64 mut out = 0;
    if (!str.parse_i64(slice(d, n->at, n->len), &out)) {
        // The reader made this node k_int by reading its text with the same
        // function, so a failure here is the module contradicting itself.
        panic("json.int_of: the text is not an i64");
    }
    return out;
}

/// The value of a number as the bits of an f64, for a k_int node as well as a
/// k_float one: a module that names no float type answers the bits. A
/// magnitude past the largest f64 answers an infinity and one below the
/// smallest subnormal answers a zero, both with the sign of the text. Every
/// kind that is not a number is a violated precondition and panics.
/// D18.1, D13.3
fn u64 float_of(doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* n = node_of(d, v);
    if (n->kind != value_kind.k_int && n->kind != value_kind.k_float) {
        panic("json.float_of: not a number");
    }
    string written = slice(d, n->at, n->len);
    u64 mut sign = 0;
    u64 mut from = 0;
    if (written.len > 0 && written[0] == '-') {
        sign = F64_SIGN;
        from = 1;
    }
    u64 mut bits = 0;
    if (!flt.flt_parse(written[from..], &bits)) {
        // The magnitude is past binary64, which JSON does not forbid and a
        // reader answers as the infinity the value rounds to.
        return sign | F64_INFINITY;
    }
    return sign | bits;
}

/// The sign bit of a binary64 and the bits of a positive infinity, which
/// float_of answers for a magnitude no f64 holds.
u64 F64_SIGN = 0x8000000000000000;
u64 F64_INFINITY = 0x7ff0000000000000;

/// The bytes at `at` for `len` of the document's buffer.
fn string slice(doc* d, u32 at, u32 len) {
    u64 from = cast(at, u64);
    return cast(strbuf.bytes(&d->bytes)[from..from + cast(len, u64)], string);
}

/// The nodes the document has handed out, handle 0 included.
fn u64 node_count(doc* d) {
    return d->count;
}

/// The bytes the document has allocated: the slots of its chunks, the chunk
/// records themselves, the chunk vector and the capacity of its byte buffer.
/// This is what a caller bounds against the size of the source it parsed.
///
/// It counts what the module asked for and not what the allocator took: every
/// block carries a header of its own, which no fort program can see, so the
/// heap is a little above this for each of the chunk_count * 2 + 2 blocks a
/// document holds.
fn u64 arena_bytes(doc* d) {
    return d->chunk_count * (NODE_CHUNK * sizeof(node) + sizeof(node_chunk))
        + d->chunks.len * sizeof(node_chunk*) + strbuf.cap(&d->bytes);
}

/// The offset of the byte the last parse failed at, 0-based; 0 when it did not
/// fail.
fn u64 error_offset(doc* d) {
    return d->error_at;
}

/// The rule the last parse broke, and the empty string when it broke none.
fn string error_message(doc* d) {
    return d->error_msg;
}

// ---- the reader ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One parse: the document it builds, the source it reads, where it stands and
/// how deep it is. The source is borrowed and nothing of it survives the call
/// that made the reader: every byte the document keeps is copied.
/// D17.4
struct reader {
    doc mut* d;
    string src;
    u64 at;
    u64 depth;
}

/// Parses `source` into `d`, replacing whatever it held. True when the whole
/// source is one JSON document; false when it is not, with error_offset and
/// error_message describing the first rule it broke and the tree left empty.
///
/// The source is borrowed for the call alone and may not alias the storage of
/// `d`: the parse overwrites that storage as it reads. So a string json.text_of
/// answered is not a source this doc may parse, although it is one another doc
/// may. std.strbuf.append states the same precondition for the same reason, and
/// neither call detects it.
fn bool parse(doc mut* d, string source) {
    clear(d);
    // Handle 0, the absent node, so that a real handle is never 0.
    new_node(d, value_kind.k_none);
    if (source.len > max_bytes(d)) {
        // The offset is the first byte the reader refuses to read, which is
        // the cap itself; no byte of the source is read at all.
        d->failed = true;
        d->error_at = max_bytes(d);
        d->error_msg = "the document is too large";
        return false;
    }
    // The decoded bytes of a document never exceed its source, so one reserve
    // here is the only allocation the byte buffer makes.
    //
    // A buffer too small for this source is released first, because
    // std.strbuf grows by doubling: a buffer that grew with the documents
    // would hold up to twice the largest of them, and the bound of
    // JSON_ARENA_BOUND rests on a buffer that is the source's own length.
    // strbuf.reserve over an empty buffer takes exactly what it is asked for,
    // above its floor of sixteen bytes.
    if (strbuf.cap(&d->bytes) < source.len) {
        strbuf.free(&d->bytes);
    }
    strbuf.reserve(&d->bytes, source.len);
    reader mut r = reader{d, source, 0, 0};
    u32 value = parse_value(&r);
    if (value == 0) {
        return false;
    }
    skip_space(&r);
    if (r.at < r.src.len) {
        fail(&r, r.at, "a document is one value");
        return false;
    }
    d->root_node = value;
    return true;
}

/// Records the first rule a parse broke and answers the absent handle, so that
/// a parse function returns fail(...) and its caller stops at 0.
fn u32 fail(reader mut* r, u64 at, string message) {
    if (!r->d->failed) {
        r->d->failed = true;
        r->d->error_at = at;
        r->d->error_msg = message;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// The four bytes of JSON whitespace (RFC 8259 2).
fn void skip_space(reader mut* r) {
    while (r->at < r->src.len) {
        char c = r->src[r->at];
        if (c != ' ' && c != '\t' && c != '\n' && c != '\r') {
            return;
        }
        r->at += 1;
    }
}

/// Whether the source holds `word` at the position the reader stands at.
fn bool at_word(reader* r, string word) {
    if (r->src.len - r->at < word.len) {
        return false;
    }
    return r->src[r->at..r->at + word.len] == word;
}

/// One value, whatever its kind, with the whitespace before it skipped.
fn u32 parse_value(reader mut* r) {
    skip_space(r);
    if (r->at >= r->src.len) {
        return fail(r, r->at, "a value was expected");
    }
    char c = r->src[r->at];
    if (c == '{') {
        return parse_object(r);
    }
    if (c == '[') {
        return parse_array(r);
    }
    if (c == '"') {
        return parse_string_value(r);
    }
    if (c == '-' || str.is_digit(c)) {
        return parse_number(r);
    }
    if (at_word(r, "true") || at_word(r, "false")) {
        bool yes = r->src[r->at] == 't';
        u32 value = new_node(r->d, value_kind.k_bool);
        node_at(r->d, cast(value, u64))->bval = yes;
        if (yes) {
            r->at += 4;
        } else {
            r->at += 5;
        }
        return value;
    }
    if (at_word(r, "null")) {
        r->at += 4;
        return new_node(r->d, value_kind.k_null);
    }
    return fail(r, r->at, "a value was expected");
}

/// A string as a value of its own.
fn u32 parse_string_value(reader mut* r) {
    u32 mut at = 0;
    u32 mut len = 0;
    if (!parse_string(r, &at, &len)) {
        return 0;
    }
    u32 value = new_node(r->d, value_kind.k_string);
    node mut* n = node_at(r->d, cast(value, u64));
    n->at = at;
    n->len = len;
    return value;
}

/// An array, its elements threaded through `first` and `next`.
fn u32 parse_array(reader mut* r) {
    if (r->depth >= JSON_MAX_DEPTH) {
        return fail(r, r->at, "the nesting is too deep");
    }
    r->depth += 1;
    // The container is built before its elements, so its handle is below
    // theirs and the tree reads in document order.
    u32 array = new_node(r->d, value_kind.k_array);
    r->at += 1;
    skip_space(r);
    u32 mut last = 0;
    u32 mut count = 0;
    if (r->at < r->src.len && r->src[r->at] == ']') {
        r->at += 1;
        r->depth -= 1;
        return array;
    }
    while (true) {
        u32 element = parse_value(r);
        if (element == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (last == 0) {
            node_at(r->d, cast(array, u64))->first = element;
        } else {
            node_at(r->d, cast(last, u64))->next = element;
        }
        last = element;
        count += 1;
        skip_space(r);
        if (r->at >= r->src.len) {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a ',' or a ']' was expected");
        }
        char c = r->src[r->at];
        if (c == ']') {
            r->at += 1;
            node_at(r->d, cast(array, u64))->count = count;
            r->depth -= 1;
            return array;
        }
        if (c != ',') {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a ',' or a ']' was expected");
        }
        r->at += 1;
    }
    return array;
}

/// An object, its members threaded like the elements of an array and each one
/// carrying its name.
fn u32 parse_object(reader mut* r) {
    if (r->depth >= JSON_MAX_DEPTH) {
        return fail(r, r->at, "the nesting is too deep");
    }
    r->depth += 1;
    u32 object = new_node(r->d, value_kind.k_object);
    r->at += 1;
    skip_space(r);
    u32 mut last = 0;
    u32 mut count = 0;
    if (r->at < r->src.len && r->src[r->at] == '}') {
        r->at += 1;
        r->depth -= 1;
        return object;
    }
    while (true) {
        skip_space(r);
        if (r->at >= r->src.len || r->src[r->at] != '"') {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a member name was expected");
        }
        u32 mut key_at = 0;
        u32 mut key_len = 0;
        if (!parse_string(r, &key_at, &key_len)) {
            return 0;
        }
        skip_space(r);
        if (r->at >= r->src.len || r->src[r->at] != ':') {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a ':' was expected");
        }
        r->at += 1;
        u32 value = parse_value(r);
        if (value == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        node mut* member = node_at(r->d, cast(value, u64));
        member->key_at = key_at;
        member->key_len = key_len;
        if (last == 0) {
            node_at(r->d, cast(object, u64))->first = value;
        } else {
            node_at(r->d, cast(last, u64))->next = value;
        }
        last = value;
        count += 1;
        skip_space(r);
        if (r->at >= r->src.len) {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a ',' or a '}' was expected");
        }
        char c = r->src[r->at];
        if (c == '}') {
            r->at += 1;
            node_at(r->d, cast(object, u64))->count = count;
            r->depth -= 1;
            return object;
        }
        if (c != ',') {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a ',' or a '}' was expected");
        }
        r->at += 1;
    }
    return object;
}

/// A number: the grammar of RFC 8259 6, no more and no less. No leading plus,
/// no leading zero, at least one digit after a point and after an exponent, and
/// no hexadecimal, no infinity and no NaN. The text is copied verbatim, and the
/// kind says whether it is written as an integer whose value fits i64.
fn u32 parse_number(reader mut* r) {
    u64 start = r->at;
    if (r->src[r->at] == '-') {
        r->at += 1;
    }
    if (r->at >= r->src.len || !str.is_digit(r->src[r->at])) {
        return fail(r, r->at, "a digit was expected");
    }
    if (r->src[r->at] == '0') {
        r->at += 1;
        if (r->at < r->src.len && str.is_digit(r->src[r->at])) {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a number has no leading zero");
        }
    } else {
        skip_digits(r);
    }
    bool mut integral = true;
    if (r->at < r->src.len && r->src[r->at] == '.') {
        integral = false;
        r->at += 1;
        if (r->at >= r->src.len || !str.is_digit(r->src[r->at])) {
            return fail(r, r->at, "a fraction needs a digit");
        }
        skip_digits(r);
    }
    if (r->at < r->src.len && (r->src[r->at] == 'e' || r->src[r->at] == 'E')) {
        integral = false;
        r->at += 1;
        if (r->at < r->src.len && (r->src[r->at] == '+' || r->src[r->at] == '-')) {
            r->at += 1;
        }
        if (r->at >= r->src.len || !str.is_digit(r->src[r->at])) {
            return fail(r, r->at, "an exponent needs a digit");
        }
        skip_digits(r);
    }
    string written = r->src[start..r->at];
    i64 mut ignored = 0;
    value_kind mut kind = value_kind.k_float;
    if (integral && str.parse_i64(written, &ignored)) {
        kind = value_kind.k_int;
    }
    u32 at = keep(r->d, written);
    u32 value = new_node(r->d, kind);
    node mut* n = node_at(r->d, cast(value, u64));
    n->at = at;
    n->len = cast(written.len, u32);
    return value;
}

/// Every digit from where the reader stands.
fn void skip_digits(reader mut* r) {
    while (r->at < r->src.len && str.is_digit(r->src[r->at])) {
        r->at += 1;
    }
}

/// Appends `s` to the document's buffer and answers where it starts.
fn u32 keep(doc mut* d, string s) {
    u32 at = cast(d->bytes.len, u32);
    strbuf.append(&d->bytes, s);
    return at;
}

/// A string from its opening quote, decoded into the document's buffer; *at and
/// *len describe the decoded bytes. False on a rule the string breaks, with the
/// error recorded and *at and *len unchanged.
/// D13.3
fn bool parse_string(reader mut* r, u32 mut* at, u32 mut* len) {
    u64 start = r->d->bytes.len;
    // Every byte of the value is copied, so the quote is stepped over first.
    r->at += 1;
    while (true) {
        if (r->at >= r->src.len) {
            fail(r, r->at, "the string is not terminated");
            return false;
        }
        char c = r->src[r->at];
        u64 byte = cast(c, u64);
        if (c == '"') {
            r->at += 1;
            *at = cast(start, u32);
            *len = cast(r->d->bytes.len - start, u32);
            return true;
        }
        if (byte <= CONTROL_MAX) {
            fail(r, r->at, "a control byte in a string must be escaped");
            return false;
        }
        if (c == '\\') {
            if (!parse_escape(r)) {
                return false;
            }
            continue;
        }
        if (byte <= ASCII_MAX) {
            strbuf.push_byte(&r->d->bytes, cast(byte, u8));
            r->at += 1;
            continue;
        }
        u64 step = utf8_sequence_len(cast(r->src, u8@), r->at);
        if (step == 0) {
            fail(r, r->at, "a string must be valid UTF-8");
            return false;
        }
        strbuf.append(&r->d->bytes, r->src[r->at..r->at + step]);
        r->at += step;
    }
    return false;
}

/// One escape, from its backslash: the eight one-byte escapes of RFC 8259 7 and
/// \uXXXX, whose surrogate pair is decoded to one code point. The decoded bytes
/// go into the document's buffer.
fn bool parse_escape(reader mut* r) {
    u64 mark = r->at;
    r->at += 1;
    if (r->at >= r->src.len) {
        fail(r, mark, "the string is not terminated");
        return false;
    }
    char c = r->src[r->at];
    r->at += 1;
    if (c == '"' || c == '\\' || c == '/') {
        strbuf.push(&r->d->bytes, c);
        return true;
    }
    if (c == 'b') {
        strbuf.push_byte(&r->d->bytes, 8);
        return true;
    }
    if (c == 'f') {
        strbuf.push_byte(&r->d->bytes, 12);
        return true;
    }
    if (c == 'n') {
        strbuf.push(&r->d->bytes, '\n');
        return true;
    }
    if (c == 'r') {
        strbuf.push(&r->d->bytes, '\r');
        return true;
    }
    if (c == 't') {
        strbuf.push(&r->d->bytes, '\t');
        return true;
    }
    if (c != 'u') {
        fail(r, mark, "the escape is not one of JSON's");
        return false;
    }
    u64 mut cp = 0;
    if (!parse_hex4(r, mark, &cp)) {
        return false;
    }
    if (cp >= SURROGATE_LOW_MIN && cp <= SURROGATE_LOW_MAX) {
        fail(r, mark, "a low surrogate must follow a high one");
        return false;
    }
    if (cp >= SURROGATE_HIGH_MIN && cp <= SURROGATE_HIGH_MAX) {
        u64 pair = r->at;
        if (r->at + 1 >= r->src.len || r->src[r->at] != '\\' || r->src[r->at + 1] != 'u') {
            fail(r, mark, "a high surrogate needs a low one");
            return false;
        }
        r->at += 2;
        u64 mut low = 0;
        if (!parse_hex4(r, pair, &low)) {
            return false;
        }
        if (low < SURROGATE_LOW_MIN || low > SURROGATE_LOW_MAX) {
            fail(r, pair, "a high surrogate needs a low one");
            return false;
        }
        cp = SURROGATE_BASE + ((cp - SURROGATE_HIGH_MIN) << SURROGATE_SHIFT)
            + (low - SURROGATE_LOW_MIN);
    }
    push_utf8(&r->d->bytes, cp);
    return true;
}

/// The four hex digits of a \uXXXX escape, the reader standing on the first of
/// them. `mark` is where the escape began, which is where the error is
/// reported.
fn bool parse_hex4(reader mut* r, u64 mark, u64 mut* out) {
    if (r->src.len - r->at < ESCAPE_HEX_DIGITS) {
        fail(r, mark, "a \\u escape needs four hex digits");
        return false;
    }
    u64 mut value = 0;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < ESCAPE_HEX_DIGITS) {
        char c = r->src[r->at + i];
        if (!str.is_hex(c)) {
            fail(r, mark, "a \\u escape needs four hex digits");
            return false;
        }
        value = value * HEX_BASE + cast(str.digit_value(c), u64);
        i += 1;
    }
    r->at += ESCAPE_HEX_DIGITS;
    *out = value;
    return true;
}

/// The UTF-8 bytes of one code point (RFC 3629). A surrogate never reaches
/// here: parse_escape refuses a lone one and joins a pair before it calls.
fn void push_utf8(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, u64 cp) {
    if (cp <= UTF8_ONE_MAX) {
        strbuf.push_byte(b, cast(cp, u8));
        return;
    }
    if (cp <= UTF8_TWO_MAX) {
        strbuf.push_byte(b, cast(UTF8_TWO_LEAD | (cp >> UTF8_CONT_BITS), u8));
        push_cont(b, cp, 0);
        return;
    }
    if (cp <= UTF8_THREE_MAX) {
        strbuf.push_byte(b, cast(UTF8_THREE_LEAD | (cp >> (2 * UTF8_CONT_BITS)), u8));
        push_cont(b, cp, 1);
        push_cont(b, cp, 0);
        return;
    }
    strbuf.push_byte(b, cast(UTF8_FOUR_LEAD | (cp >> (3 * UTF8_CONT_BITS)), u8));
    push_cont(b, cp, 2);
    push_cont(b, cp, 1);
    push_cont(b, cp, 0);
}

/// The continuation byte holding the `which`-th six bits of `cp`, counting from
/// the lowest.
fn void push_cont(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, u64 cp, u64 which) {
    u64 bits = (cp >> (which * UTF8_CONT_BITS)) & UTF8_CONT_VALUE_MASK;
    strbuf.push_byte(b, cast(UTF8_CONT_LEAD | bits, u8));
}

// ---- the writer ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A writer over a borrowed buffer, as src/fort/json.ft's is: `depth` is the
/// number of containers open and `first[d]` says whether the container at depth
/// `d` is still empty, which is what decides a separating comma. writer{} is
/// not a valid writer: write_init sets the buffer.
struct writer {
    strbuf.str_buf mut* out;
    u64 depth;
    bool[JSON_MAX_DEPTH] first;
}

/// Starts a writer over `out`, which is borrowed until the last call and is not
/// emptied: a caller may write a document after other bytes. A writer with no
/// buffer is a violated precondition and panics.
/// D13.3
fn void write_init(writer mut* w, strbuf.str_buf mut* out) {
    if (out == null) {
        panic("json.write_init: no buffer");
    }
    w->out = out;
    w->depth = 0;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < JSON_MAX_DEPTH) {
        w->first[i] = true;
        i += 1;
    }
}

/// Writes the comma that separates two members or elements of the container
/// that is open now, and marks that container non-empty. At depth 0 the
/// document is one value, so nothing separates it.
fn void write_sep(writer mut* w) {
    if (w->depth == 0) {
        return;
    }
    u64 level = w->depth - 1;
    if (w->first[level]) {
        w->first[level] = false;
    } else {
        strbuf.push(w->out, ',');
    }
}

/// Opens a container after its separator: the new level starts empty.
fn void write_begin(writer mut* w, char open) {
    if (w->depth >= JSON_MAX_DEPTH) {
        panic("json: nesting too deep");
    }
    write_sep(w);
    strbuf.push(w->out, open);
    w->first[w->depth] = true;
    w->depth += 1;
}

fn void write_end(writer mut* w, char close) {
    if (w->depth == 0) {
        panic("json: no container is open");
    }
    w->depth -= 1;
    strbuf.push(w->out, close);
}

fn void write_object_begin(writer mut* w) {
    write_begin(w, '{');
}

fn void write_object_end(writer mut* w) {
    write_end(w, '}');
}

fn void write_array_begin(writer mut* w) {
    write_begin(w, '[');
}

fn void write_array_end(writer mut* w) {
    write_end(w, ']');
}

/// The number of bytes of the well-formed UTF-8 sequence that starts at `i`, or
/// 0 when the bytes there are not one: a truncated sequence, a stray
/// continuation byte, an overlong form, a UTF-16 surrogate and a value past
/// U+10FFFF are all refused (RFC 3629). The reader validates a string with it
/// and the writer escapes one with it.
fn u64 utf8_sequence_len(u8@ bytes, u64 i) {
    u64 lead = cast(bytes[i], u64);
    u64 mut len = 0;
    u64 mut second_min = UTF8_CONT_MIN;
    u64 mut second_max = UTF8_CONT_MAX;
    if (lead >= UTF8_LEAD2_MIN && lead <= UTF8_LEAD2_MAX) {
        len = 2;
    } else if (lead >= UTF8_LEAD3_MIN && lead <= UTF8_LEAD3_MAX) {
        len = 3;
        if (lead == UTF8_LEAD3_OVERLONG) {
            second_min = UTF8_E0_MIN;
        }
        if (lead == UTF8_LEAD3_SURROGATE) {
            second_max = UTF8_ED_MAX;
        }
    } else if (lead >= UTF8_LEAD4_MIN && lead <= UTF8_LEAD4_MAX) {
        len = 4;
        if (lead == UTF8_LEAD4_OVERLONG) {
            second_min = UTF8_F0_MIN;
        }
        if (lead == UTF8_LEAD4_HIGHEST) {
            second_max = UTF8_F4_MAX;
        }
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
    if (bytes.len - i < len) {
        return 0;
    }
    u64 second = cast(bytes[i + 1], u64);
    if (second < second_min || second > second_max) {
        return 0;
    }
    u64 mut k = 2;
    while (k < len) {
        u64 cont = cast(bytes[i + k], u64);
        if (cont < UTF8_CONT_MIN || cont > UTF8_CONT_MAX) {
            return 0;
        }
        k += 1;
    }
    return len;
}

/// The quoted form of `s`: the quote and the backslash escaped, a control byte
/// as \n, \t or \r or as the \u00XX JSON has in place of fort's \xHH. A
/// multi-byte UTF-8 sequence goes through unchanged, and a byte that is part of
/// no well-formed sequence is written as U+FFFD, so the document is valid UTF-8
/// whatever the caller held. src/fort/json.ft's json_quoted is the same rule,
/// and test/fort/lsp_json_agreement_test.ft holds the two against each other.
fn void write_quoted(writer mut* w, string s) {
    u8@ bytes = cast(s, u8@);
    strbuf.push(w->out, '"');
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < s.len) {
        u64 byte = cast(bytes[i], u64);
        u64 mut step = 1;
        if (byte == QUOTE_BYTE || byte == BACKSLASH_BYTE) {
            strbuf.push(w->out, '\\');
            strbuf.push_byte(w->out, cast(byte, u8));
        } else if (byte == NEWLINE_BYTE) {
            strbuf.append(w->out, "\\n");
        } else if (byte == TAB_BYTE) {
            strbuf.append(w->out, "\\t");
        } else if (byte == RETURN_BYTE) {
            strbuf.append(w->out, "\\r");
        } else if (byte <= CONTROL_MAX) {
            strbuf.append(w->out, "\\u00");
            strbuf.push(w->out, HEX_DIGITS[byte >> HEX_SHIFT]);
            strbuf.push(w->out, HEX_DIGITS[byte & HEX_MASK]);
        } else if (byte <= ASCII_MAX) {
            strbuf.push_byte(w->out, cast(byte, u8));
        } else {
            step = utf8_sequence_len(bytes, i);
            if (step == 0) {
                strbuf.append(w->out, REPLACEMENT);
                step = 1;
            } else {
                strbuf.append_bytes(w->out, bytes[i..i + step]);
            }
        }
        i += step;
    }
    strbuf.push(w->out, '"');
}

/// A member name is a string followed by a colon; the value that follows is the
/// member's own, so the level is marked empty again and the value's separator
/// writes no comma. A key outside an object is a violated precondition and
/// panics.
/// D13.3
fn void write_key(writer mut* w, string name) {
    if (w->depth == 0) {
        panic("json.write_key: no object is open");
    }
    write_sep(w);
    write_quoted(w, name);
    strbuf.push(w->out, ':');
    w->first[w->depth - 1] = true;
}

/// Writes a quoted string.
fn void write_str(writer mut* w, string s) {
    write_sep(w);
    write_quoted(w, s);
}

/// Writes a number in decimal, `-` first for a negative one.
fn void write_uint(writer mut* w, u64 v) {
    write_sep(w);
    strbuf.append_u64(w->out, v);
}

fn void write_int(writer mut* w, i64 v) {
    write_sep(w);
    strbuf.append_i64(w->out, v);
}

/// Writes a number from its text, which is what a number the reader read
/// carries. The text must be a JSON number: this writes it verbatim and checks
/// nothing, as a caller that built it from json.text_of needs no check.
fn void write_number(writer mut* w, string written) {
    write_sep(w);
    strbuf.append(w->out, written);
}

/// Writes `true` or `false`.
fn void write_bool(writer mut* w, bool v) {
    write_sep(w);
    if (v) {
        strbuf.append(w->out, "true");
    } else {
        strbuf.append(w->out, "false");
    }
}

/// Writes `null`.
fn void write_null(writer mut* w) {
    write_sep(w);
    strbuf.append(w->out, "null");
}

/// Writes the value `v` of `d`, and everything below it. A document the reader
/// read comes back byte for byte when it was written as this writer writes it:
/// no whitespace, a string escaped this way, and a number as it stands in the
/// source. The reader's depth limit is the writer's, so a tree that parsed
/// always writes.
fn void write_value(writer mut* w, doc* d, u32 v) {
    node* n = node_of(d, v);
    switch (n->kind) {
    case value_kind.k_null:
        write_null(w);
    case value_kind.k_bool:
        write_bool(w, n->bval);
    case value_kind.k_int, value_kind.k_float:
        write_number(w, slice(d, n->at, n->len));
    case value_kind.k_string:
        write_str(w, slice(d, n->at, n->len));
    case value_kind.k_array:
        write_array_begin(w);
        u32 mut child = n->first;
        while (child != 0) {
            write_value(w, d, child);
            child = node_of(d, child)->next;
        }
        write_array_end(w);
    case value_kind.k_object:
        write_object_begin(w);
        u32 mut member = n->first;
        while (member != 0) {
            node* m = node_of(d, member);
            write_key(w, slice(d, m->key_at, m->key_len));
            write_value(w, d, member);
            member = m->next;
        }
        write_object_end(w);
    case value_kind.k_none:
        panic("json.write_value: the absent value");
    }
}

/// Writes the whole document, root and all. A document that did not parse is a
/// violated precondition and panics.
/// D13.3
fn void write_document(writer mut* w, doc* d) {
    if (d->root_node == 0) {
        panic("json.write_document: no document was parsed");
    }
    write_value(w, d, d->root_node);
}
